# assigned.cjs: function viaAssign() {} Object.assign(module.exports, { viaAssign });   main.js: import { viaAssign } from "./assigned.cjs"; viaAssign()
# Nobody knows the names of assigned.cjs before it has run, so main.js imports it through a module of its own, whose body evaluates the file and whose names the loader fills from what the file exports.
# What Object.assign copies onto an object is written nowhere on it: it lies on the source. The loader reads the own properties of what the variable of the file holds into the variables of the importers,
# so it finds the function there, and the call through the variable is a call of it. A second copy, from an object that was itself copied onto, is found as well
